      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 3 y \\cot{(x )} - 8 \\sin{(2 x )}=0\\,.\n$$\n\nAssume $\\sin x\\ne 0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 3 y \\cot{(x )} - 8 \\sin{(2 x )}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 3 y \\cot{(x )}=8 \\sin{(2 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(- 3 \\cot{(x )})}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{8 \\sin{(2 x )}}_{Q(x)}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=- 3 \\cot{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=8 \\sin{(2 x )}.$",
