      ":question/id": "69cf3843-68f4-5c39-a839-13e8ef841e1e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{- 2 x + 6 y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nAssume $x\\ne 0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{- 2 x + 6 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{- 2 x}{x}+\\frac{6 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - \\frac{6 y}{x}=\\frac{- 2 x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(- \\frac{6}{x})}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{-2}_{Q(x)}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=- \\frac{6}{x}$ and $Q(x)=-2.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
